<h2 id="section-1">Count usable states rather than photographs</h2>
<p>A product may appear in several styling images without supplying all the components shown. Start by listing the exact contents. Next, identify each supported state: what changes, what remains and what additional part is needed. Finally, ask which states you would choose with clothes and activities you already have. A theoretical combination is different from a documented one, and a documented one is different from one you want.</p>
<p>Include states you would leave unchanged for weeks. Frequent swapping is not the only valid use. Conversely, if the appeal depends on changing a piece while leaving the same base in place, establish whether that particular operation is intended. Do not infer a complete mechanism from a finished photograph or assume that every base can be worn bare.</p>
<h2 id="section-4">Compare the options on equal terms</h2>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Requirement</th><th scope="col">Convertible option</th><th scope="col">Separate pieces</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Both looks at the same time</td><td>Check whether the design supplies complete simultaneous pieces</td><td>Two complete pieces may meet the need directly.</td></tr><tr><td>Different wearing positions</td><td>Confirm every base, fit and orientation</td><td>Each piece can be selected for its own position.</td></tr><tr><td>Few morning decisions</td><td>One settled configuration may work</td><td>A familiar fixed favorite may work.</td></tr><tr><td>Occasional visual change</td><td>A confirmed motif change may be useful</td><td>Switching complete pieces may be simpler.</td></tr><tr><td>Future additions</td><td>Check reference-based compatibility and total cost</td><td>Check whether an additional complete piece is actually wanted.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Neither column is a winner by definition. A well-made convertible design can be the wrong routine for one person. A drawer of separate pieces can be equally inconvenient for another. The comparison should expose your constraints before a purchase adds more choices.</p>
<h2 id="section-7">Use a complete-cost worksheet</h2>
<p>Write one line for the base, one for every required connector and one for each ornament or additional complete piece. Record what you already own separately from what you need to buy. Use current seller prices and real delivery charges; do not treat a promotional hero price as the cost of a whole system. If any price or component is unclear, leave that comparison unresolved.</p>
<p>For a fictional example, someone wants one quiet work composition and one larger evening composition. A supported two-state set may make sense. A second person wants a necklace and bracelet together at an event. A single object shown in two positions does not supply that simultaneous look. Separate pieces may meet the second task more directly even if the convertible product has more advertised possibilities.</p>
<h2 id="section-10">Include ownership effort</h2>
<p>Consider storage, identifying small parts and getting help if a component is lost. Ask what references you should retain and whether a replacement is available; do not presume it will be offered indefinitely. Think about the consequence of one base being unavailable. A shared component can be convenient, but several planned looks may depend on that one object.</p>
<p>Avoid unproven environmental or financial conclusions. Fewer pieces in a photograph do not establish lower environmental impact, and more configurations do not guarantee a lower cost per wear. You can make a personal buying decision without assigning benefits that the seller has not evidenced. Enjoyment and a workable routine are sufficient reasons to prefer one option.</p>
<h2 id="section-13">Supporting checks for this decision</h2>
<p>The examples below are comparison tools, not SUIJI product prices, certifications or test results. Apply the questions to the actual reference you are considering.</p>
<h3 id="section-15">A photo of two earrings does not prove that two are included</h3>
<p>Hero photography communicates styling; it should not carry the entire quantity disclosure. Look for explicit language near the price and add-to-cart button:</p>
<ul><li>“one single earring”;</li><li>“one pair / two earrings”;</li><li>“one left and one right earring” if the designs differ;</li><li>“decorative element only; base not included”;</li><li>a complete, itemised box-content line.</li></ul>
<p>If those words are missing, ask before paying. Screenshots and image count are weak substitutes for a written offer.</p>
<h3 id="section-19">Why brands sell earrings individually</h3>
<p>Single-unit sales can support curated ears, multiple piercings, asymmetry and replacement. They can also lower the first number shown on a collection card. Neither model is inherently misleading; ambiguity is the problem.</p>
<p>A useful listing explains the reason without hiding the transaction. For a replacement, it should also say whether sides are identical and whether colour or finish can vary between production batches.</p>
<h3 id="section-22">The four quantities that must not be confused</h3>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">What it should mean</th><th scope="col">Question to ask</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Earring</td><td>one complete wearable piece</td><td>Is the back included?</td></tr><tr><td>Pair</td><td>two complete earrings</td><td>Are left and right identical?</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td>structural part required by a system</td><td>Can it be worn by itself?</td></tr><tr><td>Element/module</td><td>removable decorative part</td><td>Which base is compatible?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Do not call a decorative element a “single earring” if it cannot be worn without another required component.</p>
<h3 id="section-25">Interchangeable systems need an itemised offer</h3>
<p>For an interchangeable system, identify the base, decorative element, required back or connector, and complete wearing configuration. Ask which parts are included in the particular offer. A product attribute such as Core-Lock does not replace a reference-based answer about the combination you want.</p>
<h3 id="section-27">Compare prices on an equal basis</h3>
<p>Suppose one collection card shows €39 for a single and another €62 for a pair. The lower displayed number is not the lower price for two ears. Normalise the comparison:</p>
