<h3 id="section-30">Edge cases worth checking</h3>
<h3 id="section-31">Gifts</h3>
<p>A gift recipient may not know they need a separate base. A first-purchase CTA should lead to a complete compatible configuration, not a component that cannot be worn alone.</p>
<h3 id="section-33">Replacements</h3>
<p>State whether one lost side can be replaced, whether proof of purchase is required, and whether the replacement can differ slightly. Do not promise permanent availability.</p>
<h3 id="section-35">Curated ears</h3>
<p>Single sales are useful when a shopper intentionally chooses different pieces. Filters should distinguish single, pair and component so “single” does not mix complete earrings with non-wearable modules.</p>
<h3 id="section-37">Returns and hygiene</h3>
<h3 id="section-38">A plain-language check before buying</h3>
<p>Ask yourself three questions before buying: “How many wearable earrings arrive?”, “What else must I own?”, and “What would two ears cost?” If you must infer an answer from a photograph, ask for clarification. Look for a number and an object: “1 single earring,” “1 pair (2 earrings),” or “2 decorative elements; compatible base required.” Compare that meaning with the basket rather than assuming a familiar word describes a complete arrangement.</p>
<p>If you read the offer in another language, check the count again. English “set,” French “lot,” and a multiplication sign can remain ambiguous without named contents. A change of wording should not leave you guessing whether the selected object or quantity has changed.</p>
<h2 id="section-41">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="section-42">Are convertible pieces better for a small collection?</h3>
<p>Sometimes. They help when the actual states cover your needs without adding unwanted complexity. A small collection of separate favorites can work just as well if that better suits your habits.</p>
<h3 id="section-44">Is cost per wear a reliable calculation?</h3>
<p>Future wear is an estimate. You can compare scenarios, but do not present an imagined number of uses as a guaranteed saving or a reason to exceed a comfortable budget.</p>
<h3 id="section-46">Should all my jewelry belong to one system?</h3>
<p>No. Start with the pieces you enjoy. Confirm compatibility when you want a specific connection, without assuming your whole collection needs to become one interchangeable system.</p>
<h3 id="section-48">What should decide a close comparison?</h3>
<p>Choose the arrangement you can explain and picture using: complete contents, supported fit, manageable handling and a cost you accept. A longer feature list does not resolve those personal decisions.</p>
